The guard is the standard syntax-file idiom: if some syntax is already
active for the buffer, a second [[:runtime syntax/noweb.vim]] must not
redefine everything on top of it.  The [[version < 600]] branch is
fossil compatibility kept verbatim from v1.2.

<<bail out if a syntax is already active>>=
" Remove any old syntax stuff hanging around
if version < 600
  syntax clear
elseif exists("b:current_syntax")
  finish
endif
@

\subsection{The \TeX{} base}

Like CWEB's [[cweb.vim]], we treat the whole file as \TeX{} and punch
the code chunks out of it, so there is no \enquote{documentation
region}---prose is simply the top level.  The [[unlet]] afterwards
matters: [[tex.vim]] sets [[b:current_syntax]], and if we left it set,
our own guard-style checks further down the runtime path would think
the buffer already done; we are not finished defining the syntax yet.

<<load the \TeX{} base syntax>>=
" Like in CWEB, a NOWEB source file is treated as a TeX file
" including code chunks in between.
if version < 600
  source <sfile>:p:h/tex.vim
else
  runtime! syntax/tex.vim
  unlet b:current_syntax
endif
@

\subsection{Quoting and chunk names in prose}
\label{sec:quoting}

Noweb prose quotes code as [[[[...]]]] and may mention chunks as
[[@<<name@>>]]; both get their own items.  Two details are load-bearing:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[oneline]]: ] an unbalanced [[[[]] in prose used to open a
  region that ran to the end of the file and painted everything after
  it as [[Constant]].  Noweb's quoting is one-line in practice, so
  bounding the item costs nothing and removes the failure mode.
\item[ [[containedin=tex.*]]: ] these items are top-level, but \TeX{}
  regions opened in prose (optional arguments, groups, math) would
  otherwise hide the markup inside them.  Being injectable into every
  [[tex]]-prefixed group keeps the markup visible wherever prose ended
  up.  The v1.3 code named [[tex.*Zone]] groups here---groups which
  vimtex, the \TeX{} syntax actually loaded on this machine, does not
  even define.
\end{description}

<<quoting and chunk-name markup>>=
" containedin=tex.* lets these match even when a tex region has swallowed
" the surrounding prose (e.g. an unclosed optional-argument [ ... ), which
" vimtex can open across lines via nextgroup chains.
syn region  nowebTT     start="\[\["hs=s+2 end="\]\]"he=e-2 oneline containedin=tex.*
syn region  nowebName   start="@<<" end="@>>" oneline contains=nowebTT containedin=tex.*
@

\subsection{Recovering quoting stolen by option arguments}
\label{sec:orphan}

One adversary cannot be beaten by definition order: [[nextgroup]].
After [[\begin{quote}]] or [[\item]], the \TeX{} syntax expects an
optional argument and claims the next [[[]] via a [[nextgroup]]
chain---and a [[nextgroup]] match outranks \emph{every} ordinary item,
so [[nowebTT]] never gets to compete for the first bracket of a
[[[[...]]]] quote there.  If the quoted text then contains a \%, the
\TeX{} comment rule eats the closing brackets and the option region
stays open---in one real file it swallowed 147 lines of prose and
several chunks.

We recover \emph{inside} the stolen bracket instead, where ordinary
priority applies again: this contained match consumes the orphaned
[[[content]]] (spanning any \%, because an item that starts earlier
beats one that starts later), so scanning resumes exactly at the
trailing [[]]] and the option region closes on the same line.  The
lookahead is the safety catch: only a [[[X]]]]-shaped text---noweb
quoting seen from inside a stolen bracket---matches, never a genuine
\LaTeX{} [[[options]]] argument.

<<recover quoting stolen by \TeX{} option arguments>>=
" When [[...]] quoting follows a tex command taking an optional argument
" (\begin{env}, \item, ...), the tex syntax claims the first [ as the
" argument's opening delimiter via a nextgroup chain, which has priority
" over any ordinary item -- and a % inside the quoting then comments out
" the ]], leaving the option region open for the rest of the file.
" Recover from inside the stolen bracket: consume the orphaned [content]
" (which only exists in [X]] shapes, hence the lookahead) so the trailing
" ] closes the option region on the same line.
syn match nowebTTOrphan /\[[^][]*\]\]\@=/hs=s+1,he=e-1 contained containedin=tex.*Opt,tex.*Label
@

\subsection{The fallback chunk region}
\label{sec:fallback}

This region is the skeleton's safety net: it matches \emph{every}
chunk, leaves the body unhighlighted, and is outranked by the
language-aware regions that \cref{sec:refresh} layers on top (they are
defined later; definition order is priority).  Its shape, however, is
exactly the shape those regions reuse, so the reasoning lives here.

\begin{description}
\item[ [[matchgroup=nowebName]] on the start:] the header line gets
  chunk-name colours, and---the real point---contained items cannot
  match inside [[matchgroup]] text.  The shell syntax's here-doc rule
  matches [[@<<word]]; without the matchgroup it fired on the header
  itself and painted the whole chunk as one unterminated here-doc
  string.
\item[Two end patterns:] a chunk ends at the next text chunk
  ([[@]] plus space or newline) \emph{or} implicitly at the next chunk
  definition---exactly the two terminators notangle accepts.  A tab
  after [[@]] is deliberately \emph{not} an end: notangle treats that
  line as code, and the highlighting should not diverge from the
  tangler.
\item[ [[me=s-1]] on both ends:] the terminator itself stays
  unconsumed, so the following item---[[nowebStartText]] or the next
  chunk's region---starts on that very line.  (The v1.3 code used
  [[me=e-3]] on a two-character match and relied on Vim clamping the
  offset; it happened to work.)
\item[ [[keepend]]: ] included items must not push past the end match;
  without it a multi-line string in a chunk body could swallow the
  terminator.
\item[ [[containedin=tex.*]]: ] a chunk header must \emph{always} start
  a chunk, even when a runaway \TeX{} region (previous subsection) has
  swallowed the surrounding prose.  This is safe because the \TeX{}
  option regions lack [[keepend]], so a contained chunk region
  legitimately obscures their end patterns---a [[]]] inside code does
  not chop the chunk.
\end{description}

<<fallback code-chunk region>>=
" NOWEB code chunks are defined by @<<chunk_name@>>= and ended by the next
" "@" (not a "@@"!) in the first column of a line, or implicitly by the
" next chunk definition.  Both ends use me=s-1 so the terminator itself is
" left for the following item (nowebStartText or the next chunk region).
" The header is highlighted via matchgroup, which also keeps items of an
" included language syntax from matching inside it.  This is the fallback
" region for chunks whose language could not be inferred: its body is
" left unhighlighted (chunk references are still marked).  Language-aware
" regions are layered on top of it by noweb#refresh() below, which is why
" they take priority -- they are defined later.  nowebChunkRef is also
" defined there, after the language includes, so that it outranks them.
" containedin=tex.* guarantees a chunk header always starts a chunk, even
" inside a runaway tex region (see nowebTT above); the tex regions lack
" keepend, so a contained chunk safely obscures their end patterns.
syntax region nowebCode
      \ matchgroup=nowebName start=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/
      \ matchgroup=NONE
      \ end=/^@\%( \|$\)/me=s-1
      \ end=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/me=s-1
      \ keepend
      \ contains=nowebChunkRef,@NoSpell
      \ containedin=tex.*
@

\subsection{Text-chunk starts and highlight links}

The [[@]] that opens a text chunk gets a marker of its own---the ends
above left it unconsumed for exactly this item.

<<mark text-chunk starts>>=
" Here, we mark the beginning of a new text chunk.
syntax match nowebStartText /^@\%( \|$\)/ containedin=tex.*
@

The links are plain [[hi def link]] with no guard around them.  The
v1.2 code wrapped them in a global [[did_noweb_syntax_inits]] flag, a
pre-Vim-6 relic with a modern cost: after [[:syntax off | syntax on]]
the syntax file re-runs but the flag is still set, so the links were
never restored and the noweb colours silently vanished for the rest of
the session.  [[hi def link]] is idempotent; the guard protected
nothing.

<<default highlight links>>=
" The default methods for highlighting. Can be overridden later.
hi def link nowebStartText Constant
hi def link nowebTT     Constant
hi def link nowebTTOrphan nowebTT
hi def link nowebName     Type
hi def link nowebChunkRef nowebName
@

\subsection{Bootstrapping the language inference}

The rest of the file hands over to \cref{sec:autoload}.  The two
buffer variables are reset here, unconditionally: a full re-source
(e.g.\ [[:edit]]) starts the syntax from scratch, wiping the included
clusters, so stale bookkeeping claiming a language is already included
would make [[contains=@nowebLang_x]] silently match nothing.  Refresh
then runs once immediately and again after every write; the
buffer-local [[:NowebSyncLang]] covers everything in between.

<<language-inference bootstrap>>=
let b:current_syntax = "noweb"

" Infer chunk languages and build the per-language regions, then keep them
" up to date: on every load (here), after each write, and on demand via
" :NowebSyncLang.  A full re-source (e.g. :edit) wipes included clusters,
" so reset the bookkeeping before the initial inference.
let b:noweb_included = {}
let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
call noweb#refresh()

command! -buffer NowebSyncLang call noweb#refresh()

augroup nowebLangSync
  autocmd! * <buffer>
  autocmd BufWritePost <buffer> call noweb#refresh()
augroup END
@

\section{Language inference}
\label{sec:autoload}

This file is a Vimscript port of the \emph{algorithm} at the core of
noweb's \enquote{autolang} filter (from \texttt{tominted.nw}): seed
every chunk with a language taken from a filename-like chunk name or a
[[#!]] line, then propagate those languages along \emph{use} edges, so
a descriptively-named chunk used by a typed root inherits the root's
language.  Extension-only dispatch would miss most chunks in a real
document---the whole point of noweb is that chunks are named for what
they mean, not what file they land in.

The seeding \emph{data}, however, is not ported: on Neovim the
questions \enquote{what language is a file with this name?} and
\enquote{what language does this shebang name?} are answered by the
editor's own filetype database (\cref{sec:askeditor}), which is
exactly the data we would otherwise re-encode by hand.  The small
fixed tables that seeded versions 1.3--1.4 remain only as a fallback
for editors without that database.

The file reads data first, then helpers, then the algorithm---which
happens to be pedagogical order as well:

<<[[autoload/noweb.vim]]>>=
<<autoload file header>>

<<[[s:lexers_by_name]] table>>

<<[[s:lexers_by_ext]] table>>

<<[[s:interpreters]] table>>

<<the [[s:CONFLICT]] sentinel>>

<<[[s:filetype_match]]>>

<<[[s:lexer_for_chunk]]>>

<<[[s:shebang_lang]]>>

<<[[s:re_escape]]>>

<<[[s:infer_languages]]>>

<<[[s:include_lang]]>>

<<[[noweb#refresh]]>>
@

<<autoload file header>>=
" autoload/noweb.vim -- per-chunk language inference for noweb sources
"
" Ports the algorithm of the "autolang" filter from noweb's tominted.nw:
" every code chunk is seeded with a language from a filename-like chunk
" name or a #! line, and those languages then propagate along @<<use@>>
" edges (a worklist with conflict detection), so a descriptively-named
" chunk used by a typed root inherits the root's language.  On Neovim
" the seeds come from the editor's own filetype database
" (vim.filetype.match); fixed tables remain as the fallback.  The result
" drives one :syntax region per language, each including that language's
" own syntax file on demand.
@

\subsection{Asking the editor first}
\label{sec:askeditor}

Everything this file wants to know about names and shebangs is data
the editor already maintains: Neovim's filetype database maps
filenames \emph{and} file contents to filetypes, and filetypes are
precisely the names [[:syntax include]] wants.  Asking it instead of
keeping our own tables removes the redundancy, keeps the answers in
sync with the editor across upgrades, and widens coverage to every
language Neovim can detect---[[x.zsh]] becomes [[zsh]], not the
[[sh]] our table used to approximate it with.

Edit-time inference may now disagree with weave-time autolang in
corner cases (autolang asks Pygments, which calls the same chunk
[[bash]]); that is by design, not drift: each side answers how
\emph{its} medium highlights the chunk.

The results are cached because chunk names recur on every refresh and
the database does not change within a session.  On anything that is
not Neovim the function answers [['']], which routes every caller to
its fallback table.

<<[[s:filetype_match]]>>=
" Ask Neovim's filetype database (vim.filetype.match) for ARGS, e.g.
" {'filename': ...} or {'filename': ..., 'contents': [...]}.  Returns
" '' when there is no answer or no database (not Neovim).
let s:ft_cache = {}
function! s:filetype_match(args) abort
  if !has('nvim')
    return ''
  endif
  let l:key = string(a:args)
  if !has_key(s:ft_cache, l:key)
    let s:ft_cache[l:key] = luaeval('vim.filetype.match(_A) or ""', a:args)
  endif
  return s:ft_cache[l:key]
endfunction
@

\subsection{Naming a chunk's language}

The fallback tables carry the name-based seeding when the editor's
database is unavailable: exact basenames (the Makefile family has no
extension) and extensions.  The values are Vim syntax names, which
double as the canonical language keys everywhere else in this file.

<<[[s:lexers_by_name]] table>>=
" Chunk name (verbatim basename) -> Vim syntax name.
let s:lexers_by_name = {
      \ 'Makefile': 'make', 'makefile': 'make', 'GNUmakefile': 'make',
      \ }
@

<<[[s:lexers_by_ext]] table>>=
" Chunk name extension -> Vim syntax name (the canonical language key).
let s:lexers_by_ext = {
      \ '.py': 'python', '.pyw': 'python',
      \ '.c': 'c', '.h': 'c',
      \ '.cpp': 'cpp', '.cc': 'cpp', '.cxx': 'cpp', '.C': 'cpp',
      \ '.hpp': 'cpp', '.hh': 'cpp', '.hxx': 'cpp',
      \ '.sh': 'sh', '.bash': 'sh', '.zsh': 'sh', '.ksh': 'sh',
      \ '.ml': 'ocaml', '.mli': 'ocaml',
      \ '.mk': 'make',
      \ '.pl': 'perl', '.pm': 'perl',
      \ '.rb': 'ruby', '.lua': 'lua', '.php': 'php',
      \ '.js': 'javascript', '.mjs': 'javascript', '.ts': 'typescript',
      \ '.java': 'java', '.r': 'r', '.R': 'r',
      \ '.vim': 'vim', '.awk': 'awk', '.sed': 'sed',
      \ '.hs': 'haskell', '.go': 'go', '.rs': 'rust',
      \ '.sql': 'sql', '.html': 'html', '.css': 'css',
      \ '.tex': 'tex', '.yml': 'yaml', '.yaml': 'yaml',
      \ }
@

The lookup mirrors autolang's [[lexer_for_chunk]]: a chunk name may
use noweb's [[[[...]]]] quoting and may contain directory separators,
and only the basename decides---[[@<<src/util.py@>>]] is Python no
matter where it tangles to.  Note the quote-stripping must come
first; [[[[Makefile]]]] should be looked up as a basename.  The
basename, not the full name, also goes to the editor's database: its
path-based patterns (matching e.g.\ files under [[etc/]]) make no
sense for chunk names.

<<[[s:lexer_for_chunk]]>>=
" Return the Vim syntax name for chunk NAME, or '' if its name says nothing.
" The name may use noweb's [[...]] quoting and may contain directory
" separators; only the basename decides (mirrors lexer_for_chunk).
function! s:lexer_for_chunk(name) abort
  let l:name = a:name
  if l:name =~# '^\[\[.*\]\]$'
    let l:name = l:name[2:-3]
  endif
  let l:base = matchstr(l:name, '[^/]*$')
  let l:ft = s:filetype_match({'filename': l:base})
  if l:ft !=# ''
    return l:ft
  endif
  <<look [[l:base]] up in the fallback tables>>
endfunction
@

<<look [[l:base]] up in the fallback tables>>=
if has_key(s:lexers_by_name, l:base)
  return s:lexers_by_name[l:base]
endif
let l:dot = strridx(l:base, '.')
if l:dot >= 0
  let l:ext = strpart(l:base, l:dot)
  if has_key(s:lexers_by_ext, l:ext)
    return s:lexers_by_ext[l:ext]
  endif
endif
return ''
@

\subsection{Shebangs}

A chunk whose name says nothing may still open with [[#!/bin/sh]].
On Neovim the shebang goes to the editor's database as file
\emph{contents}, together with a neutral, extensionless dummy
filename---contents alone make Neovim skip parts of its detection
(a bare [[#!/bin/bash]] goes unrecognised), while an unknown filename
routes the query through the full machinery, shebang analysis
included.

The fallback interpreter table maps interpreter basenames to syntax
names, and its parser follows the [[env]] indirection because
[[#!/usr/bin/env python3]] is the portable spelling---the interesting
token is the argument, not [[env]] itself.

<<[[s:interpreters]] table>>=
" Interpreter basename from a #! line -> Vim syntax name.
let s:interpreters = {
      \ 'python': 'python', 'python2': 'python', 'python3': 'python',
      \ 'sh': 'sh', 'bash': 'sh', 'dash': 'sh', 'ksh': 'sh', 'zsh': 'sh',
      \ 'perl': 'perl', 'ruby': 'ruby', 'lua': 'lua', 'php': 'php',
      \ 'node': 'javascript', 'nodejs': 'javascript',
      \ 'awk': 'awk', 'gawk': 'awk', 'sed': 'sed', 'Rscript': 'r',
      \ }
@

<<[[s:shebang_lang]]>>=
" Return the language a #! line names, or '' if it is not a #! line.
function! s:shebang_lang(line) abort
  if a:line !~# '^#!'
    return ''
  endif
  let l:ft = s:filetype_match({'filename': 'noweb-chunk', 'contents': [a:line]})
  if l:ft !=# ''
    return l:ft
  endif
  <<parse the shebang with the fallback table>>
endfunction
@

<<parse the shebang with the fallback table>>=
" Follow the env indirection: the interesting token is env's argument.
let l:toks = split(a:line[2:])
if empty(l:toks)
  return ''
endif
let l:interp = matchstr(l:toks[0], '[^/]*$')
if l:interp ==# 'env' && len(l:toks) > 1
  let l:interp = matchstr(l:toks[1], '[^/]*$')
endif
return get(s:interpreters, l:interp, '')
@

\subsection{Escaping chunk names into patterns}

The region builder in \cref{sec:refresh} splices chunk names into a
magic-mode [[/.../]] pattern, so everything magic there must be
escaped---including the backslash first, so the escapes themselves do
not get re-escaped.

<<[[s:re_escape]]>>=
" Escape a chunk name for literal use inside a /.../ (magic) pattern.
function! s:re_escape(name) abort
  return escape(a:name, '\/.*$^~[]')
endfunction
@

\subsection{Inferring the languages}
\label{sec:infer}

The inference is one buffer scan followed by a propagation pass over
the collected graph:

<<[[s:infer_languages]]>>=
" Scan the buffer and return {chunk name -> Vim syntax name}.
function! s:infer_languages() abort
  <<scan chunks for language seeds and uses>>
  <<propagate languages along use edges>>
  return l:langs
endfunction
@

\subsubsection{The scan}

The scanner is a small state machine over the buffer lines: a chunk
header enters code mode, a text-chunk marker leaves it, and everything
in between belongs to the current chunk.  The header and terminator
patterns are the same ones the syntax regions use---inference and
highlighting must agree on what a chunk is.

The state is a handful of variables serving two different masters.
Two of them are the scan's \emph{products}: [[l:langs]] maps chunk
names to inferred languages and is what the function returns, and
[[l:uses]] is the graph the propagation pass will walk---for each
chunk, the set of chunk names its body references.  One is a
\emph{protection marker}: [[l:explicit]] records which entries of
[[l:langs]] came from the chunk's own name or shebang, so that
propagation later refuses to override an author-stated language with
an inherited one.  The rest is the state machine proper:
[[l:current]] and [[l:in_code]] say which chunk we are inside, if
any, and [[l:body_started]] remembers whether a non-blank body line
has been seen yet---only the first one may carry a shebang.

<<scan chunks for language seeds and uses>>=
let l:lines = getline(1, '$')
let l:uses = {}
let l:langs = {}
let l:explicit = {}
let l:current = ''
let l:in_code = 0
let l:body_started = 0
for l:line in l:lines
  if l:line =~# '^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$'
    <<open a chunk and seed its language from its name>>
  elseif l:in_code && l:line =~# '^@\($\| \)'
    let l:in_code = 0
    let l:current = ''
  elseif l:in_code && l:current !=# ''
    <<seed from a shebang on the first body line>>
    <<collect chunk uses on the line>>
  endif
endfor
@

A name-derived language is recorded as \emph{explicit}: the name is
the author's own statement of intent, so neither a later shebang nor
propagation may override it.

<<open a chunk and seed its language from its name>>=
let l:current = matchstr(l:line, '^@<<\zs.\{-}\ze@>>=\s*$')
let l:in_code = 1
let l:body_started = 0
if !has_key(l:uses, l:current)
  let l:uses[l:current] = {}
endif
if !has_key(l:explicit, l:current)
  let l:lx = s:lexer_for_chunk(l:current)
  if l:lx !=# ''
    let l:langs[l:current] = l:lx
    let l:explicit[l:current] = 1
  endif
endif
@

Only the first \emph{non-blank} body line can be a shebang---that is
where a kernel would look, modulo the blank lines notangle strips
between concatenated chunk definitions.

<<seed from a shebang on the first body line>>=
if !l:body_started && l:line !~# '^\s*$'
  let l:body_started = 1
  if !has_key(l:explicit, l:current)
    let l:sb = s:shebang_lang(l:line)
    if l:sb !=# ''
      let l:langs[l:current] = l:sb
      let l:explicit[l:current] = 1
    endif
  endif
endif
@

Uses are collected from every code line.  A [[@<<...@>>]] immediately
followed by [[=]] is a definition, not a use---continued definitions
of the same chunk would otherwise register the chunk as using itself,
which is harmless for propagation but pollutes the graph.

<<collect chunk uses on the line>>=
" Collect @<<use@>> references (a @<< ... @>> not followed by '=').
let l:start = 0
while 1
  let l:mp = matchstrpos(l:line, '@<<.\{-}@>>', l:start)
  if l:mp[1] < 0
    break
  endif
  let l:mend = l:mp[2]
  if l:mend < len(l:line) && l:line[l:mend] ==# '='
    let l:start = l:mend
    continue
  endif
  let l:uses[l:current][l:mp[0][2:-3]] = 1
  let l:start = l:mend
endwhile
@

\subsubsection{Propagation}

Languages flow from seeded chunks to the chunks they use, worklist
style.  Two rules keep the result trustworthy.  Explicitly-typed
chunks are immune---their name or shebang outranks any inheritance.
And a chunk reachable from two \emph{differently}-typed parents is
marked conflicted rather than first-come-first-served: a wrong
highlight is worse than none, and conflicted chunks simply keep the
plain fallback region.

<<propagate languages along use edges>>=
" Propagate languages along use edges (worklist with conflict marking).
let l:worklist = keys(l:langs)
while !empty(l:worklist)
  let l:parent = remove(l:worklist, -1)
  let l:lang = l:langs[l:parent]
  if l:lang ==# s:CONFLICT
    continue
  endif
  for l:child in keys(get(l:uses, l:parent, {}))
    if has_key(l:explicit, l:child) || s:lexer_for_chunk(l:child) !=# ''
      continue
    endif
    if !has_key(l:langs, l:child)
      let l:langs[l:child] = l:lang
      call add(l:worklist, l:child)
    elseif l:langs[l:child] !=# s:CONFLICT && l:langs[l:child] !=# l:lang
      let l:langs[l:child] = s:CONFLICT
    endif
  endfor
endwhile
@

The conflict marker only needs to be impossible as a real syntax
name; the NUL bytes guarantee that.

<<the [[s:CONFLICT]] sentinel>>=
let s:CONFLICT = "\x00conflict\x00"
@

\subsection{Including a language's syntax}
\label{sec:include}

[[:syntax include]] reads a syntax file into a cluster instead of the
buffer's top level.  The dance around [[b:current_syntax]] is forced
on us: syntax files begin with the guard we ourselves use---bail out
if [[b:current_syntax]] is set---so the variable must be unset for the
include to do anything, and restored afterwards so the buffer still
knows its own syntax is loaded.  Absence of a syntax file is cached as
an empty cluster name; there is no point globbing the runtime path for
[[fortran.vim]] on every write.

<<[[s:include_lang]]>>=
" Include LANG's syntax under cluster @nowebLang_<lang>, once per buffer.
" Returns the cluster name, or '' if Vim ships no such syntax.
function! s:include_lang(lang) abort
  let l:cluster = 'nowebLang_' . substitute(a:lang, '[^A-Za-z0-9]', '_', 'g')
  if has_key(b:noweb_included, a:lang)
    return b:noweb_included[a:lang]
  endif
  if empty(globpath(&runtimepath, 'syntax/' . a:lang . '.vim'))
    let b:noweb_included[a:lang] = ''
    return ''
  endif
  let l:saved = exists('b:current_syntax') ? b:current_syntax : ''
  if l:saved !=# ''
    unlet b:current_syntax
  endif
  execute 'silent! syntax include @' . l:cluster . ' syntax/' . a:lang . '.vim'
  if l:saved !=# ''
    let b:current_syntax = l:saved
  elseif exists('b:current_syntax')
    unlet b:current_syntax
  endif
  let b:noweb_included[a:lang] = l:cluster
  return l:cluster
endfunction
@

\subsection{Rebuilding the regions}
\label{sec:refresh}

[[noweb#refresh()]] is the entry point the syntax file calls; it
re-infers everything and rebuilds all per-language items.  Its five
phases run in a fixed order, and the last two exist purely because of
the invariants of \cref{sec:overview}: the chunk-reference item must
be defined after every include, and the sync policy must be restated
after every include.

<<[[noweb#refresh]]>>=
" Re-infer chunk languages and (re)build the per-language syntax regions.
function! noweb#refresh() abort
  <<reset per-buffer region state>>

  <<bucket chunk names by language>>

  <<build one region per language>>

  <<redefine [[nowebChunkRef]] after the includes>>

  <<sync from the start>>
endfunction
@

The reset clears the previous refresh's regions by group name.
[[syntax clear]] on a group removes its patterns but keeps the group
id valid, so items already referring to the group do not break.

<<reset per-buffer region state>>=
if !exists('b:noweb_included')
  let b:noweb_included = {}
endif
if !exists('b:noweb_lang_groups')
  let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
endif
for l:grp in b:noweb_lang_groups
  execute 'silent! syntax clear ' . l:grp
endfor
let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
@

Regions are built per \emph{language}, not per chunk---a document with
forty Python chunks needs one region with forty start alternatives,
not forty regions.  Conflicted chunks are dropped here (they keep the
fallback), and so are two languages by name: [[text]] means
\enquote{explicitly no highlighting}, and [[tex]] is skipped because
the buffer's outer syntax already \emph{is} \TeX{}---re-including it
as a cluster would load the whole \TeX{} syntax a second time and
re-run its [[syn sync]] and [[spell]] setup mid-refresh.

<<bucket chunk names by language>>=
let l:langs = s:infer_languages()
let l:bylang = {}
for [l:name, l:lang] in items(l:langs)
  " 'tex' is skipped: the buffer's outer syntax already is tex, and
  " re-including it as a cluster re-runs its sync/spell setup mid-refresh.
  if l:lang ==# s:CONFLICT || l:lang ==# 'text' || l:lang ==# 'tex'
    continue
  endif
  let l:bylang[l:lang] = get(l:bylang, l:lang, []) + [l:name]
endfor
@

Each region is the fallback region's shape (\cref{sec:overview}
explains [[matchgroup]], the two [[me=s-1]] ends and [[keepend]]) with
the language's cluster added to [[contains]].  Being defined after
the fallback, these win the header-line tie against it.

<<build one region per language>>=
let l:i = 0
for [l:lang, l:names] in items(l:bylang)
  let l:cluster = s:include_lang(l:lang)
  if l:cluster ==# ''
    continue
  endif
  let l:i += 1
  let l:grp = 'nowebCodeLang' . l:i
  let l:alt = join(map(copy(l:names), 's:re_escape(v:val)'), '\|')
  " matchgroup= keeps included items (e.g. sh.vim's here-doc rule, which
  " matches @<<word) from ever firing on the header line; me=s-1 on both
  " ends leaves the terminator unconsumed, so a following chunk header
  " both ends this region and starts the next one.
  execute 'syntax region ' . l:grp
        \ . ' matchgroup=nowebName start=/^@<<\%(' . l:alt . '\)@>>=\s*$/'
        \ . ' matchgroup=NONE'
        \ . ' end=/^@\%( \|$\)/me=s-1'
        \ . ' end=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/me=s-1'
        \ . ' keepend'
        \ . ' contains=@' . l:cluster . ',nowebChunkRef,@NoSpell'
        \ . ' containedin=tex.*'
  call add(b:noweb_lang_groups, l:grp)
endfor
@

Chunk references inside code bodies are the one noweb markup that must
survive \emph{inside} the included languages---and the shell syntax's
here-doc rule matches the same [[@<<]] text.  Winning that tie is why
this item is (re)defined here, after every include, rather than once
in the syntax file.  The pattern requires non-space first and last
name characters, so a real redirected here-doc like
[[cat @<<EOF @>> log]] is left to the shell syntax.

<<redefine [[nowebChunkRef]] after the includes>>=
" Must be (re)defined after every :syntax include above: at the same
" start position the last-defined item wins (:h syn-priority), which is
" what lets chunk refs beat e.g. shHereDoc's @<<-pattern inside sh chunks.
" First/last name chars must be non-space, so `cat @<<EOF @>> log` stays a
" here-doc.  syntax clear keeps the group id (contains= refs stay valid)
" while preventing pattern accumulation across refreshes.
silent! syntax clear nowebChunkRef
syntax match nowebChunkRef /@<<\%([^ ]\|[^ ].\{-}[^ ]\)@>>/ contained contains=nowebTT
@

Finally, the sync policy.  This line is the single biggest reliability
fix in v1.4: every included syntax file may install [[syn sync]] rules,
and those are \emph{buffer-global}---the Python syntax declares that a
line matching [[^def]] is top level, the make syntax declares that
nearly \emph{every} line is.  Scrolling then re-syncs the buffer
wrongly and chunks lose their highlighting mid-file, depending on
which language happened to be included last.  We discard all imported
sync rules and parse from the top of the file; noweb sources are small
enough that exactness costs nothing.

<<sync from the start>>=
" Included syntax files install buffer-global sync rules (pythonSync
" grouphere on ^def, make's groupthere on ^[^\t#], small minlines) that
" re-sync this buffer wrongly while scrolling.  Drop them and parse from
" the top; noweb sources are small.
syntax sync clear
syntax sync fromstart
@

\section{Building and maintaining}
\label{sec:building}

Editing this file follows the usual literate loop: edit
[[vim-noweb.nw]], run [[make]], and commit the [[.nw]] together with
the re-tangled files.  [[make check]] enforces the two things worth
enforcing: [[noroots]] must report exactly the three root chunks (an
extra root is a typo'd chunk reference), and the tangled files must
match the working tree.

